.TH MTS 1 "October 2026" "mts dev" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
mts \- modern timestamp filter, a Perl free moreutils ts replacement
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B mts
.RI [ flags ] " [" format ]
.br
.B mts
.RI [ flags ] " -- " command " [" args ...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B mts
prepends a timestamp to every line of standard input and writes the
result to standard output, like
.BR ts (1)
from moreutils but as a single static binary with millisecond startup.
.PP
In exec mode (after
.BR -- ),
.B mts
runs
.I command ,
stamps its standard output and standard error separately while keeping
the streams separate, optionally tees both to a file, forwards signals
to the child, and exits with the child's exit code.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-i
Incremental timestamps: time elapsed since the previous line.
Default format changes to
.IR "%H:%M:%S" .
.TP
.BR \-s
Since-start timestamps: time elapsed since
.B mts
started.
Default format changes to
.IR "%H:%M:%S" .
.TP
.BR \-m
Accepted for compatibility with
.BR ts (1)
command lines; changes nothing.
.TP
.BR \-r
Rewrite existing timestamps in the input as relative times
(``15m5s ago''). With a format argument, reformat them instead.
With \-i/\-s, report deltas between parsed stamps. Unparseable
lines pass through unchanged. Missing years assume the current
year, missing zones the local timezone.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-utc
Use UTC instead of local time. Mutually exclusive with
.BR \-\-tz .
.TP
.BR \-\-tz " " \fINAME\fR ", " \-\-timezone " " \fINAME\fR
Use the named timezone, such as
.IR America/Vancouver .
Overrides
.BR \-u .
Falls back to
.B $TZ,
then legacy
.B $TIMEZONE,
then local time.
Requires system zoneinfo (install the tzdata package).
.TP
.B \-\-iso8601\fR, \fB\-\-rfc3339
Format timestamps as ISO-8601 / RFC 3339 with nanoseconds.
.TP
.B \-\-prefix " " \fISTR\fR
Extra label between timestamp and line, such as
.IR "[web]" .
.TP
.B \-\-output " " \fIFILE\fR
Tee output to
.I FILE
(append unless
.B \-\-truncate
is given) in addition to the console.
.TP
.B \-\-truncate
Truncate
.B \-\-output
.I FILE
instead of appending.
.TP
.B \-\-output\-mode " " \fIMODE\fR
Exact permissions for
.B \-\-output
.I FILE
in octal, such as
.IR 640 ,
bypassing umask. Default without the flag is
.I 0644
masked by umask.
.TP
.B \-\-run\-user " " \fIUSER\fR[:\fIGROUP\fR]
Run the wrapped command as
.IR USER [: GROUP ]
(name or id \- needs privilege).
Group defaults to the primary group.
Only the primary uid and gid are set;
supplementary groups are inherited.
.TP
.B \-\-output\-user " " \fIUSER\fR[:\fIGROUP\fR]
Change ownership of
.B \-\-output
to
.IR USER [: GROUP ]
(name or id).
.I USER:GROUP
is shorthand for
.B \-\-output\-user
.I USER
plus
.B \-\-output\-group
.IR GROUP .
Defaults to
.B \-\-run\-user
when set.
.TP
.B \-\-output\-group " " \fIGROUP\fR
Change group ownership of
.B \-\-output
(name or id). Overrides the group in
.B \-\-output\-user ;
alone changes group only.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
With
.BR \-\-output ,
do not write to stdout/stderr.
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print version, copyright and license, and exit.
.SH FORMAT
The optional
.I format
argument uses
.BR strftime (3)
conversions plus the moreutils extensions
.BR %.S ,
.BR %.s
and
.B %.T
(subsecond resolution, such as
.IR 30.00001 ).
Default:
.IR "%b %d %H:%M:%S" ;
incremental default:
.IR "%H:%M:%S" .
.SH TIMEZONES
Absolute timestamps use the system local time by default
.RB ( /etc/localtime
when
.B $TZ
is unset).
Each of
.B \-\-tz ,
.B $TZ
and legacy
.B $TIMEZONE
just takes an IANA timezone name string such as
.IR America/Vancouver .
Precedence:
.B \-\-tz
>
.B $TZ
>
.B $TIMEZONE
(legacy)
>
.B \-u
> local.
.SH EXIT STATUS
Filter mode exits 0 on success, 1 on I/O errors, 2 on usage errors.
Exec mode exits with the child's exit code (128+signal when the child
is killed by a signal). A stamping/stream error is reported and exits 1
only when the child itself succeeded, so the child status is never masked.
.SH EXAMPLES
.EX
ping 10.0.0.1 | mts
make 2>&1 | mts \-i '[%H:%M:%S]'
mts \-\-tz America/Vancouver < app.log
mts \-\-prefix '[web]' \-\-output /var/log/web.log \-\- ./bin/web
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ts (1),
.BR strftime (3)
